WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.540
Whatever you think about whether&nbsp;
there are actual ghosts in "A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:07.020
Haunting in Venice," the characters&nbsp;
are clearly haunted in other ways:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:12.501
by war, by love, by failure. Here's&nbsp;
how their tragedies all tie together.

00:00:12.501 --> 00:00:14.601
Warning: Spoilers ahead!

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:19.740
"A Haunting in Venice" takes place in&nbsp;
the fall of 1947, as the Italian "City&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:24.180
of Canals" undertakes a much-needed period&nbsp;
of peace and healing following the end of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:29.040
World War II. A retired Hercule Poirot&nbsp;
enjoys the comforts of walks, pastries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:34.440
and perfectly prepared eggs, with seemingly no&nbsp;
interest in being a detective anymore. That is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:39.550
until his old acquaintance, mystery writer&nbsp;
Ariadne Oliver approaches him with a proposition.

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:44.160
Ariadne has been invited to seance at a&nbsp;
local palazzo. The world-famous medium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:48.060
Joyce Reynolds has been hired to contact&nbsp;
the spirit of the late Alicia Drake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:52.963
a young woman who seemingly killed herself&nbsp;
after a prolonged period of mental illness.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.046 --> 00:00:58.386
Alicia's mother Rowena, her housekeeper&nbsp;
Olga, family doctor Leslie Ferrier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.386 --> 00:01:04.200
and Alicia's ex-fiance Maxime Gerard are among&nbsp;
the others in attendance. Upon Ariadne's request,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:07.740
Poirot attends the seance to try&nbsp;
and prove that Reynolds is a fake.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.686
"Spot the con I can't."

00:01:09.686 --> 00:01:14.100
He identifies some tricks in her act,&nbsp;
but things get more serious when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:19.740
medium is thrown from a balcony and impaled on&nbsp;
a statue below. As a storm besieges the palazzo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:24.000
Poirot locks the gates and begins to&nbsp;
investigate. As he too begins to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.020
ghosts in the supposedly cursed house,&nbsp;
he struggles to identify the truth.

00:01:29.589 --> 00:01:32.580
As Poirot investigates the&nbsp;
deaths of Drake and Reynolds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:36.720
he makes a number of shocking discoveries.&nbsp;
He interviews the medium's assistants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:42.180
half-siblings Nicholas and Desdemona&nbsp;
Holland. He also speaks with Dr. Ferrier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:46.665
who suffers from debilitating&nbsp;
PTSD, and his creepy son Leopold.

00:01:46.665 --> 00:01:52.080
Dr. Ferrier falls into a violent episode and&nbsp;
is subsequently locked in the palazzo's nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:56.760
soundproof music room for the supposed&nbsp;
safety of all involved. Poirot deduces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:01.620
that Ariadne was actually in cahoots with Ms.&nbsp;
Reynolds the whole time. Along with Poirot's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:06.870
bodyguard Vitale Portfoglio, who's revealed to&nbsp;
be the police officer who found Alicia's body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:10.320
Ariadne helped Ms. Reynolds and her&nbsp;
assistants rig all the tricks for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:14.760
seance. Her goal was to stump Poirot and&nbsp;
make him admit to the medium's powers.

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:17.811
"Just admit that you are up&nbsp;
against something bigger than you!"

00:02:17.811 --> 00:02:20.820
Ariadne was planning to write&nbsp;
a hit book about Reynolds to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:23.128
return to the top of the literary establishment.

00:02:23.128 --> 00:02:28.020
As Poirot interrogates Ariadne, Dr.&nbsp;
Ferrier is found dead. The legend of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:31.800
the palazzo says that it's haunted by the&nbsp;
ghosts of orphan children who were killed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:35.940
by brutal doctors and nurses. Some&nbsp;
assume that Dr. Ferrier's death is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:40.560
proof of the supernatural, but Poirot has&nbsp;
another explanation. He reveals that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:45.413
grieving mother Rowena Drake is actually the&nbsp;
one responsible for the whole bloody affair.

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:51.060
Alicia Drake suffered from a psychological&nbsp;
illness after being dumped by Maxime. She&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:55.020
claimed to see ghosts in the house, but&nbsp;
in reality, her affliction was nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:59.050
supernatural. Jealous and terrified&nbsp;
of losing her daughter to a husband,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:05.220
Rowena caused the split and concocted a plan to&nbsp;
keep her daughter by her side in perpetuity. Using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:11.046
a special poison grown in her honey garden, she&nbsp;
kept Alicia sickly and sedated by spiking her tea.

00:03:11.046 --> 00:03:13.866
When Olga volunteered to&nbsp;
watch Alicia for the night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.866 --> 00:03:18.360
she unknowingly gave her a fatal dose&nbsp;
of the drug. To cover up her evil deed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:22.920
Rowena threw her daughter off the balcony and&nbsp;
left the mark of the "Children's Vendetta" on her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:28.080
invoking the supposed curse. She may not have&nbsp;
meant for Alicia to die, but she willingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:33.450
harmed her for weeks, purely out of jealousy,&nbsp;
and made it very easy for an accident to happen.

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:36.092
"She came back to tell me that she was murdered."

00:03:36.092 --> 00:03:41.040
Poirot discovers that Rowena is being blackmailed&nbsp;
by someone who knows her secret. After the seance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:44.400
she kills Reynolds and changes one of&nbsp;
the clocks to give herself an alibi.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:49.080
She either suspects that the medium is the&nbsp;
blackmailer or fears that a second seance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:52.620
will uncover the truth. It's eventually&nbsp;
revealed that the medium was running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:57.752
an entirely separate con. The blackmail of&nbsp;
Rowena came from a much less obvious place.

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:02.520
Poirot briefly suspects that Dr.&nbsp;
Ferrier was blackmailing Rowena,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:07.380
the woman he loved, but who never returned&nbsp;
his affections. His autopsy of Alicia should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:11.886
have revealed that she died from poison, not&nbsp;
drowning. But his actions weren't malicious.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.886 --> 00:04:16.620
The doctor's declining mental condition&nbsp;
caused him to miss the obvious, or perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:22.500
he was blinded by love. That said, someone did&nbsp;
notice the poisoning: Ferrier's son, Leopold.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:27.300
The young boy was the one blackmailing Rowena,&nbsp;
in a scheme to provide for himself and his dad.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:32.580
After his father's death and the resolution&nbsp;
of the case, the boy is taken in by Olga. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:37.440
laments what happened, but Poirot assures him&nbsp;
that it isn't his fault. No child should have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:42.775
to be a parent to their parents, and the scars of&nbsp;
the war forced him into an impossible position.

00:04:42.775 --> 00:04:46.920
Rather than keep the remaining blackmail&nbsp;
money, Leopold and Olga decide to give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:51.936
it to Nicholas and Desdemona so that they&nbsp;
can travel to St. Louis, as is their dream.

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:57.660
Poirot explains that the death of Dr. Ferrier&nbsp;
was an elaborate manipulation orchestrated by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:03.300
Rowena Drake. The combination of intense&nbsp;
PTSD and unrequited love for Rowena leaves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:06.840
the doctor in a highly vulnerable&nbsp;
state, and she convinces him over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:11.788
the inner-house telephone to kill himself,&nbsp;
making it look like a supernatural event.

00:05:11.788 --> 00:05:15.900
There are a few different reasons why she&nbsp;
does this. Rowena is clearly paranoid about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:19.440
her blackmailer, and she may think&nbsp;
it's the doctor, since he was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:23.820
one who performed the autopsy. Or maybe she&nbsp;
fears that he will return to his notes after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:28.740
all of the commotion and unearth the truth.&nbsp;
Regardless, his isolated death makes a strong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:33.448
case for the ghosts being real, which would&nbsp;
theoretically absolve Rowena of culpability.

00:05:33.448 --> 00:05:37.680
The doctor's death is perhaps the saddest&nbsp;
in the whole film. Leopold tries so hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:41.160
to care for his father in a world&nbsp;
that's utterly unprepared to deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:45.600
with the mental effects of the war. When&nbsp;
he sees him lying dead, all he can say is,

00:05:45.765 --> 00:05:47.265
"Why did he have to be alone?"

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:53.100
Ariadne Oliver isn't guilty of murder,&nbsp;
but Poirot does catch her in a devious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:56.220
scheme. She tries to use him as&nbsp;
a tool in her literary comeback,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.220 --> 00:06:00.366
even bringing his bodyguard into the&nbsp;
plan to help make Reynolds seem legit.

00:06:00.366 --> 00:06:04.380
At the beginning of the movie, she tells Poirot&nbsp;
that her last three books have been critically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:09.120
panned. She also claims to be responsible for&nbsp;
making the detective famous in the first place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:15.240
by publicizing an early case of his. She argues,&nbsp;
then, that she has every right to use him. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:20.100
two don't exactly part on the best of terms, but&nbsp;
Poirot doesn't seem to hold a grudge against her.

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:25.020
Ariadne is a mainstay of Agatha Christie's&nbsp;
fiction and frequently served as a stand-in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:29.640
for the author herself. Her arc in "A Haunting&nbsp;
in Venice" is a good deal different from her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:33.300
role in the book the film is based on,&nbsp;
"Hallowe'en Party," but there is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:37.140
interesting connection. The novel received&nbsp;
generally negative reviews upon release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:41.520
and was written off by many as proof&nbsp;
that Christie was past her prime. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:46.294
interesting to see Ariadne dealing with a&nbsp;
similar dilemma in "A Haunting in Venice."

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:52.500
After Poirot catches Rowena in her lies, she&nbsp;
confronts him on a balcony. As the storm rages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:57.480
Poirot catches a glimpse of what appears to be a&nbsp;
ghost in the lightning. It's the shape of Alicia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.480 --> 00:07:01.200
hanging in the air, looming behind&nbsp;
her mother. In the next moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:05.730
Rowena loses her balance and plummets off&nbsp;
the balcony to her death. The audience and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:10.249
Poirot are left to ponder if she was blown&nbsp;
off by the wind or pushed by a spirit.

00:07:10.249 --> 00:07:14.460
Poirot discovers that his own hallucinations&nbsp;
around the house were caused by Rowena's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:19.020
poisonous honey. Or were they? Leopold repeatedly&nbsp;
says that he talks to the ghosts of children&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:23.640
in the palazzo. Some of them, he claims,&nbsp;
are even his friends. He has no reason to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:27.720
think that Alica's death was supernatural,&nbsp;
but he insists that the haunting is real.

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:31.140
Is Poirot still being affected by&nbsp;
the honey when he sees Rowena fall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:36.420
or does he actually see a ghost on the balcony?&nbsp;
The secret basement of the palazzo confirms the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:40.980
evil deeds and numerous deaths that took place&nbsp;
there. Even Poirot can't explain the whole thing.

00:07:41.434 --> 00:07:43.594
"Someone else is in this house."

00:07:43.594 --> 00:07:47.400
Poirot leaves the palazzo the next&nbsp;
morning a seemingly changed man.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:51.060
He has a new passion for detective&nbsp;
work and immediately returns to it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:55.980
accepting a case that was proposed to him in&nbsp;
the opening scene. Why does a man so firmly in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.980 --> 00:08:00.480
retirement leap back to his old job after&nbsp;
his experience at the palazzo? Maybe it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:04.225
because this particular brush with death&nbsp;
makes him reexamine how he wants to live.

00:08:04.225 --> 00:08:08.640
At the beginning of the film, Poirot openly&nbsp;
admits that he has no friends, and it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:12.763
hard to imagine that solitude is always a good&nbsp;
thing for him. Reynolds tells him at one point,

00:08:12.763 --> 00:08:14.743
"We know the dead too well, I think."

00:08:14.743 --> 00:08:18.660
Though her own powers as a medium are&nbsp;
left ambiguous, Poirot seems to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:22.680
her statement to heart. His line of work&nbsp;
is not a happy one, but it is a calling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:28.456
and he returns to it with renewed vigor. He seems&nbsp;
decidedly driven to start actively living again.

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:34.320
"A Haunting in Venice" is a murder mystery and a&nbsp;
ghost story, but it's also about the aftermath of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:39.720
World War II. By moving "Hallowe'en Party" from&nbsp;
its original English setting to Italy in 1947,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:45.000
director and star Kenneth Branagh and&nbsp;
screenwriter Michael Green reorient the focus.

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:49.980
Dr. Ferrier suffers because of the things he&nbsp;
saw in the war, and this psychological wound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:54.420
causes him to misdiagnose Alicia's&nbsp;
cause of death. Because of his PTSD,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:59.027
his son is forced to grow up extraordinarily&nbsp;
quickly and become the adult in the family.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.027 --> 00:09:03.840
This pushes him to make the questionable decision&nbsp;
of blackmailing Rowena instead of coming forward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:08.520
with evidence against her. With his father&nbsp;
unable to work, what choice does he have?

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:13.080
The Holland half-siblings are also scarred by&nbsp;
the war. They want to go to St. Louis because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:17.700
they saw it in a movie that the American soldiers&nbsp;
who saved their lives showed them. Their whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:22.800
conception of a happy life is determined by their&nbsp;
horrific experiences in war-torn Europe. There are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:27.300
bigger ghosts that haunt the story beyond those&nbsp;
said to be in the palazzo. Venice, Italy, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:31.680
the world at large are trying to pull themselves&nbsp;
together after an unfathomable amount of violence.

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:37.320
The Hollywood labor union strikes prohibit&nbsp;
writers and actors from marketing upcoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:40.740
projects, so there hasn't been much&nbsp;
promotion from the cast and crew of "A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:45.540
Haunting in Venice." But back in April&nbsp;
2023, before either strike had begun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:48.780
Kenneth Branagh did speak with The&nbsp;
Hollywood Reporter about the film.

00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:52.320
He couldn't say much about the movie's&nbsp;
ending and spoil the surprises,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:57.120
but Branagh did suggest some interesting things&nbsp;
about potential sequels. When asked if he'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:01.800
faced studio pressure to unite his Poirot films&nbsp;
under a single franchise title for marketing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:06.600
Branagh said he hadn't "thus far." His phrasing&nbsp;
suggests that the director and star has plans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:10.920
for even more Agatha Christie mysteries,&nbsp;
which he seemed to confirm later on, saying,

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:14.280
"The movies are what they are, and&nbsp;
if it evolves in a different way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:18.120
we'll cross those particular bridges&nbsp;
when we come to them. But so far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:20.760
I've just been left to get on&nbsp;
with the titles as they are."

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:24.540
That certainly leaves the door wide open&nbsp;
for more Poirot cases on the big screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:28.427
provided "A Haunting in Venice"&nbsp;
performs well enough to justify them.

00:10:29.263 --> 00:10:34.123
Branagh isn't the only one who sounds optimistic&nbsp;
about more Poirot mysteries coming down the line.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.123 --> 00:10:39.180
Prior to the release of "A Haunting in Venice,"&nbsp;
executive producer James Prichard spoke with Total&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.180 --> 00:10:44.340
Film about the possibility of more sequels set&nbsp;
in Agatha Christie's mystery universe, saying,

00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:49.140
"If Ken [Branagh] wants to do more, and Michael&nbsp;
[Green] wants to write more, we'll certainly do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.140 --> 00:10:53.912
another. There's a lot of material still to go,&nbsp;
so we're not going to run out of inspiration."

00:10:53.912 --> 00:10:58.440
With Christie's whole library available for&nbsp;
potential adaptation, Branagh's series could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:03.600
indeed go on for as long as he wants, provided the&nbsp;
box office returns are there. However, Prichard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:07.020
who is Christie's great-grandson&nbsp;
and CEO of her literary estate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.020 --> 00:11:10.800
emphasized that it's also important&nbsp;
to keep things fresh, saying,

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:15.600
"If we are going to continue to make these&nbsp;
films, we can't do the same thing over and over."

00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:19.620
"A Haunting in Venice" is definitely a bit of&nbsp;
a shift from "Murder on the Orient Express"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:24.634
and "Death on the Nile," throwing the mystery&nbsp;
more into the supernatural thriller category.

00:11:24.634 --> 00:11:28.200
"Which is pretty exciting, because it&nbsp;
involves him, and us, being scared."

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:33.720
It would also be great to see Tina Fey return as&nbsp;
Ariadne Oliver. If audiences respond well to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:38.534
film and its many changes to Christie's work,&nbsp;
future sequels could get even more creative.

